{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773329956,"nonce":"ynXXhkY82KakSBWo+FaL+Ti1FMc=","title":"Post lifecycle summary comments to git-bug issues","message":"## Summary\n\nWhen a session reaches key lifecycle events, the watcher should post a brief summary comment to the git-bug issue. This gives visibility into agent reasoning without overwhelming the tracker.\n\n## Events to post\n\n- **Start**: \"Session started, working on: \u003cbrief plan\u003e\"\n- **Resume**: \"Resumed — reason: context limit / stall / max-turns. Checkpoint count: N\"\n- **Question**: \"Blocked on question: \u003cquestion text\u003e\"\n- **Done**: \"Session complete. Checkpoints: N. View logs: `nightshift logs \u003cid\u003e` / `nightshift history \u003cid\u003e`\"\n- **Reject/Revise**: \"Sent back for revision: \u003creason\u003e\"\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Add comment posting in `host/watcher/` at lifecycle transitions (qa_handler, review_orchestrator, session_monitor)\n- Read summary from session files (state.json, conversation.jsonl) — don't parse raw logs\n- Use `tracker.add_comment(issue_id, message)` — the watcher already has tracker access\n- Keep comments short (3-5 lines max) to avoid git-bug bloat\n- Include `nightshift logs \u003cid\u003e` and `nightshift history \u003cid\u003e` commands for full trace\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Only the watcher posts (never the container — StaticTracker stays read-only)\n- Batch: don't post on every poll cycle, only on state transitions\n- git-bug lock contention: use the existing retry logic in GitBugTracker._run()\n\n## Requirement\n\nREQ-002 (Session lifecycle), REQ-008 (Notifications)","files":null}]}